config TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG
	bool "PCIe LTSSM training log"
	help
	  Record PCIe LTSSM state transitions into a CSM ring buffer that the
	  host can read back, for debugging link training failures. The buffer
	  address is published to scratch register 24 and its size to 25.

	  Blackhole is a PCIe endpoint, so on switch-attached boards the link
	  is trained by host BIOS long after PCIe init runs. The recorder
	  therefore busy-polls indefinitely and never returns, which means no
	  init step after PCIe runs: the chip parks at post code 8 and never
	  reaches GDDR training (9), eth (A), the SMBus target (B), tensix
	  init (D) or the host message queue, so there is no telemetry and no
	  fan control. Both host readers reach the buffer with raw JTAG or
	  NOC reads rather than the message queue, so they still work.

	  This produces a debug firmware for capturing link training, not a
	  bootable one, so it is default off and must be enabled explicitly.

if TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG

config TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG_ENTRIES
	int "LTSSM log capacity in entries"
	default 256
	range 8 4096
	help
	  Number of transitions the ring holds, shared across both PCIe
	  instances. Each entry occupies 16 bytes of CSM. The ring retains the
	  most recent transitions, and consecutive repeats of a state - or of a
	  two-state alternation such as Detect churn - are collapsed into a
	  repeat count rather than consuming entries, so the default covers far
	  more elapsed time than its entry count suggests.

endif # TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG
